+# Prepared command publication batches
+
+This contract owns `src/publication_batch.rs`, the certified command publisher
+in `src/server.rs`, and the unpublished WAL buffer in `src/wal.rs` under
+`packages/tofu-db/`. Authority admission, optimistic entity validation, receipts,
+WAL framing, checkpoint CONTROL and ownership remain their existing authorities.
+
+## Problem and decision
+
+Independent prepared commands previously reacquired authority one by one and
+wrote one WAL record per filesystem call. Strict responses already wait outside
+authority; moving fsync alone cannot remove this publication and thread-handoff
+cost. A caller now leads a bounded FIFO cohort using the runtime's shared
+admission handle. There is no additional worker, cache or launch override.
+
+Each member retains its existing private preparation lease. The leader holds
+authority; member callers confirm live connections and current deadlines before
+their transaction is taken. The leader also checks cancellation and monotonic/
+wall deadlines, then runs
+ordinary `AuthorityDatabase::commit` in cohort order. Business and owner fences,
+commit-owned index/outbox maintenance, sequence assignment, stream cursors and
+receipt behavior run for each member. Same-key conflicts keep their existing
+fallback after the cohort is written; a cohort is not an unchecked transaction
+union and does not require a single owner or tenant.
+
+At most the WAL protocol's 64 members share one authority acquisition. Singletons keep the direct write path. Only
+Strict logs with an attached barrier buffer writes. The ephemeral buffer holds
+at most the existing 8-MiB encoded-group limit and splits larger cohorts while
+preserving independently framed, hash-chained records. Direct-engine and Normal
+barriers keep their existing behavior. Worker/connection and prepared-pin
+budgets bound queued members from launch resources; cancellation removes its
+queue entry and releases untaken preparation immediately.
+
+## Publication and failures
+
+Buffered bytes are unpublished. No query can acquire the authority and no
+successful response or notice escapes before the final write completes.
+Checkpoints and CONTROL publication flush and sync the pending prefix first;
+a successor generation covers old witnesses only through the existing durable
+checkpoint protocol. Every successful member waits outside authority on the
+final written generation witness. A crash may recover an unacknowledged prefix;
+no member claims durability from a logical buffered length alone.
+
+A failed/torn flush latches Engine restart-required and rejects the entire
+cohort. A previously latched uncertainty discards remaining buffered bytes;
+it never retries them or acknowledges earlier in-memory successes. Existing
+member conflicts/errors remain individual when the cohort flush succeeds.
+Removed cohort members receive an immediate failure on unwinding; cancellation
+and deadline outcomes remain explicit. A publication that already started may
+finish after disconnect, as with the existing authority path; receipts resolve
+that ambiguous client outcome.
+
+## Measurement
+
+`publication_queue_wait` spans caller completion waiting; its exclusive part
+excludes work performed by a leader. `publication_batch_execute` measures that
+leader's whole cohort; `publication_authorize_wait` exposes the caller-confirmation
+rendezvous inside it. Per-member profile attribution is suspended during
+cohort work so a leader does not pretend to own other callers' reads/writes.
+Authority-hold duration for a leader includes its cohort. Process/component
+stages still record member work and `publication_batch_flush`; global batch
+counters expose completed cohorts, attempted members and maximum observed cohort size. Neither
+summed waiting nor cohort wall time is CPU time. Structural byte and member
+ceilings are bounds, not low-resource concurrency defaults.
+
+Alternatives considered: deleting FIFO sacrifices bounded admission/fairness;
+merging writes without sequential business validation loses OCC; publishing
+before writing changes recovery and error behavior; a dedicated permanent
+publisher adds worker lifecycle and resource costs. Caller-led publication
+retains the existing executor and durability boundary while amortizing writes.
